Regression tests for the buffer manager's HOT/COOL cooling-stage eviction
policy (src/backend/storage/buffer/freelist.c and bufmgr.c).

Covers the properties that motivated removing BufferAccessStrategy for
VACUUM/big sequential scans/COPY: a fresh admission starts COOL (not
HOT), promotion to HOT requires a genuine second touch, and eviction
always prefers an already-COOL buffer over demoting a HOT one. Observed
and controlled entirely through contrib/pg_buffercache's usagecount
column and eviction helper functions -- no changes to the buffer manager
itself.

test_bufmgr_eviction.conf's settings exist specifically to make the
eviction test deterministic, not just for speed:

- shared_buffers = 8MB: small enough that a modestly-sized flood
  relation can force real reclaim cycles through the whole pool, but
  large enough that ordinary catalog overhead from the test's own
  setup queries doesn't evict the tracked blocks before the flood
  even runs. Too small and the test's own bookkeeping perturbs the
  very state it's trying to observe.
- bgwriter_lru_maxpages = 0: BgBufferSync's own demand-driven
  pre-cooling runs on its own schedule (bgwriter_delay, default
  200ms) regardless of whether the foreground sweep needs a HOT
  victim, and this test's whole run is well under that. Left enabled
  it was the confirmed cause of ~25% intermittent failures -- a
  second, unrelated source of HOT->COOL demotion that has nothing to
  do with the foreground "always prefer COOL" property under test.
  Disabling it made the test 10/10 stable.
- autovacuum = off, checkpoint_timeout = 1h: remove other background
  sources of buffer touches during the test's brief (sub-second)
  window, so nothing but the test itself moves the tracked blocks.
